[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFirst, let me recall some facts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. In the category of varieties (or schemes), a surjective regular map $\pi: X [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\to Y$ is an epimorphism in some categorical sense.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. The question is whether the factorization through $\pi$ preserves regularity.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mKey considerations:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Case when $\pi$ is the quotient map / categorical quotient:**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf $\pi: X \to Y$ is a surjective regular map, and $\rho: X \to Z$ is constant [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mon the fibers of $\pi$ (which is what $\rho = f \circ \pi$ means - $\rho$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfactors through $\pi$ as a set map), then is $f$ regular?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is related to the concept of "geometric quotient" or "categorical [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mquotient."[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Important counterexample consideration:**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mConsider the Frobenius map in characteristic $p$. Let $k$ be an algebraically [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mclosed field of characteristic $p > 0$. Let $\pi: \mathbb{A}^1 \to \mathbb{A}^1$[0m[38;2;124;124;124mbe the Frobenius map $x \mapsto x^p$. This is a surjective regular map (since [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$k$ is algebraically closed, every element has a $p$-th root). The map $\pi$ is [0m[38;2;124;124;124ma bijection on $k$-points but not an isomorphism.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow consider $\rho = \text{id}: \mathbb{A}^1 \to \mathbb{A}^1$. We have $\rho = [0m[38;2;124;124;124mf \circ \pi$ where $f$ is the set-theoretic inverse of Frobenius, i.e., $f(y) = [0m[38;2;124;124;124my^{1/p}$ (the unique $p$-th root). As a set map, $f$ exists because $\pi$ is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mbijection on $k$-points. But $f$ is NOT a regular map, because $y^{1/p}$ is not [0m[38;2;124;124;124ma regular function (the Frobenius map is not an isomorphism).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, let me verify this more carefully. $\pi: \mathbb{A}^1 \to \mathbb{A}^1$ by[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$x \mapsto x^p$. This is surjective (algebraically closed field). $\rho = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\text{id}: \mathbb{A}^1 \to \mathbb{A}^1$. Then $\rho = f \circ \pi$ means $x = [0m[38;2;124;124;124mf(x^p)$, so $f(y) = y^{1/p}$ (the unique $p$-th root of $y$ in $k$). This $f$ is[0m[38;2;124;124;124ma well-defined set map $k \to k$. But $f$ is not a regular map because $k[y^{1/[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mp}] \neq k[y]$ as subrings of $k(y)$ — the function $y^{1/p}$ is not in $k[y]$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the answer is **No**, it is not necessarily true.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut wait — the problem says "quasi-projective varieties." We need to be careful [0m[38;2;124;124;124mabout the base field. If the field is characteristic 0, the Frobenius argument [0m
