[38;2;124;124;124m\ldots\}$ — actually, using $y^2 = x^3$, we can reduce any monomial to one with [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$y$ to at most the first power: monomials are $x^i$ and $x^i y$ for $i \geq 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf $y/x \in k[Y]$, write $y/x = \sum a_i x^i + \sum b_i x^i y$. Then $y = x [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\cdot (\sum a_i x^i + \sum b_i x^i y) = \sum a_i x^{i+1} + \sum b_i x^{i+1} y$. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mThe left side is $y = 0 \cdot 1 + 0 \cdot x + 1 \cdot y + 0 \cdot x^2 + \ldots$.[0m[38;2;124;124;124mThe right side is $\sum a_i x^{i+1} + \sum b_i x^{i+1} y$. Comparing [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcoefficients of $x^i$ and $x^i y$:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m- Coefficient of $1$ (i.e., $x^0$): left = 0, right = 0 (since $x^{i+1}$ with [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$i+1 \geq 1$). ✓[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Coefficient of $x$: left = 0, right = $a_0$. So $a_0 = 0$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Coefficient of $y$: left = 1, right = 0 (since $x^{i+1} y$ with $i+1 \geq 1$).[0m[38;2;124;124;124mSo $1 = 0$. Contradiction![0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore $y/x \notin k[Y]$, and $f$ is not regular.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mGreat, so this is a valid counterexample in characteristic 0.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me reconsider. The problem says "quasi-projective varieties." The [0m[38;2;124;124;124mcuspidal curve $Y = \{y^2 = x^3\} \subset \mathbb{A}^2$ is an affine variety, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mhence quasi-projective. $\mathbb{A}^1$ is also quasi-projective. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnormalization map $\pi: \mathbb{A}^1 \to Y$ is a surjective regular map. So this[0m[38;2;124;124;124mworks.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me also verify the Frobenius example works for completeness, but the cusp [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexample is cleaner and works in characteristic 0.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the answer is **No**.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write up the proof cleanly.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Proof:**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe show the answer is **No** by providing a counterexample.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet $k$ be an algebraically closed field (of any characteristic, but let's take [0m[38;2;124;124;124mcharacteristic 0 for concreteness). [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet $Y$ be the cuspidal curve in $\mathbb{A}^2_k$ defined by $y^2 = x^3$, i.e., [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$Y = \text{Spec } k[x,y]/(y^2 - x^3)$. This is an affine variety, hence quasi-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mprojective.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet $X = \mathbb{A}^1_k = \text{Spec } k[t]$, also quasi-projective.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mDefine $\pi: X \to Y$ by $t \mapsto (t^2, t^3)$. This is a regular map [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcorresponding to the ring homomorphism $k[x,y]/(y^2 - x^3) \to k[t]$ sending $x [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\mapsto t^2, y \mapsto t^3$. This is well-defined since $(t^2)^3 = t^6 = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(t^3)^2$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$\pi$ is surjective: For any point $(a, b) \in Y$ with $b^2 = a^3$, if $a \neq [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m0$, take $t = b/a$ (then $t^2 = b^2/a^2 = a^3/a^2 = a$ and $t^3 = b^3/a^3 = b [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\cdot b^2/a^3 = b \cdot a^3/a^3 = b$). If $a = 0$, then $b = 0$, and $t = 0$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmaps to $(0,0)$. So $\pi$ is surjective.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mMoreover, $\pi$ is bijective on $k$-points: if $t_1^2 = t_2^2$ and $t_1^3 = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mt_2^3$, then $t_1 = t_1^3/t_1^2 = t_2^3/t_2^2 = t_2$ (when $t_1 \neq 0$; when [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$t_1 = 0$, $t_2^2 = 0$ so $t_2 = 0$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow let $Z = \mathbb{A}^1_k$ and let $\rho = \text{id}_X: X \to Z$ be the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124midentity map.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince $\pi$ is bijective on $k$-points, there is a unique set map $f: Y \to Z$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msuch that $\rho = f \circ \pi$, namely $f = \pi^{-1}$ (the set-theoretic [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minverse), which sends $(t^2, t^3) \mapsto t$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe claim $f$ is **not** a regular map.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf $f$ were a regular map $Y \to \mathbb{A}^1$, it would correspond to a regular[0m[38;2;124;124;124mfunction on $Y$, i.e., an element of the coordinate ring $k[Y] = k[x,y]/(y^2 - [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mx^3)$. On the normalization, this function would pull back to $t \in k[t]$, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhich equals $y/x$ (since $y/x = t^3/t^2 = t$ on the open set where $x \neq 0$, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mand by density this extends).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo we need $y/x \in k[Y]$. Suppose for contradiction that $y/x = g(x,y)$ for [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msome $g \in k[Y]$. Then $y = x \cdot g(x,y)$ in $k[Y]$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIn $k[Y] = k[x,y]/(y^2 - x^3)$, every element can be uniquely written as $p(x) +[0m[38;2;124;124;124mq(x) \cdot y$ where $p, q$ are polynomials in $x$ (using the relation $y^2 = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mx^3$ to eliminate higher powers of $y$). Write $g = p(x) + q(x) y$. Then:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$$x \cdot g = x \cdot p(x) + x \cdot q(x) \cdot y$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor this to equal $y = 0 + 1 \cdot y$, we need:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $x \cdot p(x) = 0$ as a polynomial in $x$, which forces $p = 0$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $x \cdot q(x) = 1$ as a polynomial in $x$, which is impossible (no polynomial [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$q$ satisfies $x \cdot q(x) = 1$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a contradiction. Therefore $y/x \notin k[Y]$, and $f$ is not a regular [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmap.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThus, we have exhibited a surjective regular map $\pi: X \to Y$ between quasi-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mprojective varieties, a regular map $\rho: X \to Z$, and a set map $f: Y \to Z$ [0m[38;2;124;124;124mwith $\rho = f \circ \pi$, such that $f$ is not regular.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe answer is **No**.[0m

